# catalyst quickstart

**Target: 15 minutes, on a stopwatch.** Start it when you begin step 1 and
note the time at the end of each step — [`FEEDBACK.md`](FEEDBACK.md) asks
for it.

You will install catalyst into a fresh repository, document one rule, fix
the code that breaks it, commit the fix with a traced message, and check
the result. Every command is meant to be pasted as is, in order, into one
shell (bash or zsh). No agent is needed: this is the path the agent
follows for you, done by hand, so you can see each piece.

## 0. Before the clock starts

You need:

- `python3` 3.9 or later and `git`, with `git config user.name` and
  `user.email` set;
- a catalyst checkout, with its CLI built into `dist/catalyst.pyz`;
- a checkout of the process module the trial uses,
  `catalyst-software-engineering` (listed in catalyst's
  `framework/modules/catalog.md`).

```sh
git clone git@github.com:oliben67/catalyst.git
git clone git@github.com:oliben67/catalyst-software-engineering.git
cd catalyst && PYTHONPATH=scripts python3 -c 'from pathlib import Path; import package_release as p; print(p.build_cli(Path("."), Path("dist/catalyst.pyz")))' && cd ..
```

(`task build:cli` does the same if you have Task.) Then set these once, in
the shell you will use — adjust the two paths and your name:

```sh
CATALYST="$PWD/catalyst"
MODULE="$PWD/catalyst-software-engineering"
ME="Ada Lovelace"
ME_GIT=ada
```

## 1. A fresh repository (1 minute)

A one-function program whose greeting is wrong on purpose.

```sh
mkdir greet && cd greet
git init -q
printf 'def greet(name):\n    return "hello " + name\n' > greet.py
git add greet.py && git commit -qm "chore: project skeleton"
```

## 2. Install catalyst (2 minutes)

```sh
python3 "$CATALYST/dist/catalyst.pyz" init --name greet --module software-engineering \
  --user "$ME" --git-username "$ME_GIT" --rule-doc greeting-rules:gr \
  --kernel "$CATALYST/framework/kernel" --module-dir "$MODULE"
catalyst() { python3 "$PWD/.criterion/bin/catalyst.pyz" "$@"; }
catalyst hook install
git add greet.catalyst .gitignore && git commit -qm "chore: install catalyst"
```

What happened: `init` built the working copy in `.criterion/` (rules,
entity folders, your user, an empty journal, the vendored CLI) and wrote
`greet.catalyst`, the one file the repository tracks. `.criterion/` is
gitignored and has its own git history. `hook install` added a
`commit-msg` hook: from now on every commit must cite an artifact or rule
ID, or start `chore:` — as these two did. The `catalyst` function saves
typing; run everything from `greet/`.

## 3. Document the first rule (5 minutes)

A rule lives in a **domain**, registered first; its ID comes from the CLI.

```sh
cat > .criterion/rules/domains/gr-GREET-greeting-output.md <<EOF
# \`GREET\` — Greeting output

**Document:** rules/greeting-rules.md
**Name:** greeting-output
**Defined:** $(date +%F)
**Parent:** none
**Sub-domains:** none

## Scope

What the greeting says, and how it is written.

## Relationship to other domains

None.
EOF
echo "| [\`GREET\`](gr-GREET-greeting-output.md) | rules/greeting-rules.md | $(date +%F) |" >> .criterion/rules/domains/domains.md
RULE=$(catalyst id next-rule gr GREET) && echo "$RULE"
```

Now the rule itself, in the rule document, and its line in the global
rules index:

```sh
cat > .criterion/rules/greeting-rules.md <<EOF
# Greeting rules

Prefix: \`gr\`.

## Contents

- [\`GREET\`](#greeting-output) — \`$RULE\`

## Greeting output

> **Domain:** \`GREET\` — see [domains/gr-GREET-greeting-output.md](domains/gr-GREET-greeting-output.md).

### \`$RULE\` The greeting is capitalised and punctuated

✅ working. \`greet("Ada")\` returns \`Hello, Ada!\`. Implemented: \`greet.py\`.

## Linked Artifacts — Quick Index

*(none yet)*
EOF
cat > .criterion/rules/rules.md <<EOF
# Rules index

## Documents

| Prefix | Document | Domains |
|---|---|---|
| \`gr\` | [\`greeting-rules.md\`](greeting-rules.md) | \`GREET\` |

## Rule IDs

- \`$RULE\` — The greeting is capitalised and punctuated
EOF
catalyst journal append --command "rule edit" --action create --artifact "$RULE" --target "$RULE" \
  --intent "Document how the greeting must read, so the code has a rule to answer to." \
  --file .criterion/rules/greeting-rules.md --file .criterion/rules/rules.md \
  --file .criterion/rules/domains/domains.md --file .criterion/rules/domains/gr-GREET-greeting-output.md
catalyst check
```

`journal append` recorded who changed which files, why, and each file's
content hash before and after. `check` should pass with no errors.

## 4. A traced fix (4 minutes)

`greet.py` breaks the rule you just wrote, so this change restores a
documented rule's behaviour: a **fix**. With this module, a fix is a bug
report targeting the rule.

```sh
BUG=$(catalyst id next BUG) && echo "$BUG"
BUG_FILE=.criterion/development/bugs/${BUG%-*}-greeting-not-capitalised.md
cat > "$BUG_FILE" <<EOF
# \`$BUG\` — Greeting is not capitalised

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **ID** | \`$BUG\` |
| **Name** | \`greeting-not-capitalised\` |
| **Status** | Closed |
| **Severity** | Low |
| **Opened** | $(date +%F) |
| **Targets** | \`$RULE\` |
| **Domain** | \`GREET\` |
| **Area** | \`greet.py\` |
| **Steps** | *(none)* |
| **Signed-off-by** | $ME |

## Description

\`greet("Ada")\` returns \`hello Ada\`; \`$RULE\` requires \`Hello, Ada!\`. Fixed in \`greet.py\`.
EOF
printf 'def greet(name):\n    return f"Hello, {name}!"\n' > greet.py
catalyst index regen
catalyst journal append --command /create-bug --action create --artifact "$BUG" --target "$RULE" --tier fix \
  --intent "Restore $RULE: the greeting was lower-case and unpunctuated." \
  --file greet.py --file "$BUG_FILE" --file .criterion/development/bugs/bugs.md
```

## 5. Commit with a traced message, and check (3 minutes)

First, an untraced commit — the hook refuses it:

```sh
git add greet.py
git commit -m "fix the greeting"
```

Then the same commit, citing the bug (the short form, without your userid,
is enough):

```sh
git commit -qm "${BUG%-*}: capitalise and punctuate the greeting"
catalyst trace HEAD
catalyst check
catalyst report
```

`trace HEAD` checks every commit in the history: three, all traced.
`check` passes. `report` shows the journal entries, the tiers, `commits
traced: 3/3` and the closed bug.

**Stop the clock.**

## What you have

- `greet.py` line 2 answers "why is it like this?": its commit cites the
  bug, the bug targets the rule, the rule says what the greeting must be,
  and the journal holds each file's exact before-and-after.
- `python3 .criterion/bin/catalyst.pyz journal restore <timestamp> /tmp/greet-then`
  rebuilds every recorded file as it stood at any moment.
- In real work your agent does steps 3 and 4 for you (`/create-bug`, the
  rule edit), and you review them.

Now fill in [`FEEDBACK.md`](FEEDBACK.md). The full picture: the example
project in the release distribution, and `framework/kernel/CLI.md`.
